VI  - 7
IP  - 3
DP  - 2014 Sep 15
TI  - Iatrogenic tracheobronchial rupture.
PG  - 343-8
AB  - Iatrogenic tracheobronchial ruptures most frequently occur during tracheal 
      intubation, but they can also be produced during tracheobronchial endoscopy or 
      thoracic surgery. The clinical presentation can be brutal, with respiratory 
      failure, cervical emphysema, pneumothorax and hemoptysis. There are also less 
      symptomatic presentations. The diagnosis is confirmed by bronchoscopy. The 
      therapeutic approach can be differentiated, surgical or conservative, although 
      the criteria are not universally accepted. This article aims to review the 
      indications and therapeutic options.
