TI  - Pulmonary Mycobacterium kansasii Infection Mimicking Malignancy on the (18)F-FDG 
      PET Scan in a Patient Receiving Etanercept: A Case Report and Literature Review.
PG  - 973573
LID - 10.1155/2014/973573 [doi]
LID - 973573
AB  - A 66-year-old male presented with chest pain, malaise, generalized weakness, and 
      weight loss. He had been receiving etanercept injection for rheumatoid arthritis. 
      Chest X-ray revealed a right upper lobe mass. Chest computed tomography (CT) 
      showed a right apical mass, highly suggestive of a Pancoast tumor. The thoracic 
      fluorine-18 fluoro-deoxy-glucose ((18)F-FDG) positron emission tomography (PET) 
      scan demonstrated significantly high metabolic pulmonary lesions with the 
      standardized uptake value (SUV) of 12.5, consistent with lung cancer. The patient 
      underwent bronchoscopy and bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL). BAL cytology was 
      negative for malignant cells. BAL acid fast bacilli (AFB) smears were positive, 
      and Mycobacterium kansasii was eventually isolated. He received a 12-month course 
      of rifampin, isoniazid, and ethambutol. Interval resolution of pulmonary lesions 
      was noted on follow-up serial CT chest studies. There has been increasing 
      incidence of nontuberculous mycobacterial infections reported in patients treated 
      with the antitumor necrosis factor-alpha (anti-TNF-alpha) agents. Infectious foci 
      have an increased glucose metabolism which potentially causes a high FDG uptake 
      on the (18)F-FDG PET scan, leading to undue anxiety and cost to the patients. 
      This is the first reported case of pulmonary M. kansasii infection with a 
      positive thoracic (18)F-FDG PET study mimicking malignancy in a patient on 
      etanercept.
