DP  - 2014
TI  - Supraglottic Hemangioma as a Rare Cause of Recurrent Hemoptysis: A New Treatment 
      Modality with Argon Plasma Coagulation (APC).
PG  - 50-2
AB  - Adult laryngeal hemangiomas are rare and mainly involve the supraglottic region. 
      Most common symptoms include dysphagia, dysphonia, shortness of breath and 
      occasionally recurrent bleeding which sometimes cause a diagnostic dilemma for 
      pulmonologists and head and neck surgeons. There is no consensus about the most 
      appropriate treatment modality for laryngeal hemangiomas. We present a case of 
      supraglottic hemangioma in an adult female, which was diagnosed by fiberoptic 
      bronchoscopy and coagulated by Argon plasma coagulation (APC) with no 
      complication.
