VI  - 24 Suppl 3
DP  - 2014 Nov
TI  - Coexisting pulmonary tuberculosis and unsuspected bronchial foreign body removed 
      by fiberoptic bronchoscopy after 42 years.
PG  - S270-1
AB  - Aspiration of foreign bodies into the tracheobronchial tree is extremely rare in 
      adults without an underlying predisposing factor. A high index of suspicion is 
      the most important factor leading to a diagnosis of tracheobronchial foreign body 
      aspiration. Unless the patients give a clear history of aspiration, a foreign 
      body may remain occult for years. To our knowledge, the longest bronchial foreign 
      body retention in an adult recorded in English literature is 40 years. We present 
      a case of coexisting pulmonary tuberculosis and unsuspected foreign body which 
      remained in the bronchus of an adult for 42 years before the diagnosis.
