DP  - 2014 May-Aug
TI  - Successful emergency airway management in a case of removal of foreign body 
      bronchus in a pediatric patient.
PG  - 250-2
LID - 10.4103/0259-1162.134525 [doi]
AB  - Foreign body (FB) aspiration into the respiratory tract is a common incident, 
      especially in the pediatric age group and can, sometimes, pose a real challenge 
      to the anesthesiologists as far as the airway management is concerned. Here, we 
      report a case of FB bronchus in a 3 year 2 months old boy, presenting to the 
      emergency in a cyanosed and comatosed condition with severe respiratory distress. 
      In spite of the unavailability of a pediatric fiberoptic bronchoscope in our 
      hand, the gradual declining condition of the baby made us to take the challenge. 
      The FB was successfully removed through a tracheotome using a nasal endoscope.
