TI  - Tracheomediastinal fistula caused by non-Hodgkin's lymphoma.
PG  - 599-601
LID - 10.5761/atcs.cr.12.02230 [doi]
AB  - Non-Hodgkin's lymphoma involving the tracheobronchial tree is uncommon. A 
      60-year-old man presented with severe cough for two months. Bronchoscopy 
      disclosed an ulcerative lesion at the lower trachea and carina with anterior 
      tracheomediastinal fistula, and biopsy revealed tracheal wall B-cell lymphoma. 
      After chemo-radiotherapy, the tracheomediastinal fistula healed, but eight months 
      later, tracheal stenosis was diagnosed by bronchoscopy. A self-expansible 
      metallic stent was placed, which successfully maintained the airway.
