TI  - Aquatic leech as a rare cause of respiratory distress and hemoptysis.
PG  - 85-6
AB  - This paper presents a 73 years old male with three weeks history of intermittent 
      hemoptysis, dysphagia, dyspnea, stridor, and suffocations sensation. By means of 
      fibrotic bronchoscopic examination, the cause was found to be a leech in the 
      glottis. It was removed by injection of 4 ml lidocaine 2%. Infestation into the 
      respiratory tract by a leech may become lethal because of hypoxia and death 
      secondary to airway obstruction. A high index of suspicion of leech infestation 
      sould be considered in patients presenting with hemoptysis, hoarseness and 
      respiratory distress and a history of recent contact with fresh water streams.
