TI  - Current and emerging imaging in relation to drug discovery in airways disease.
PG  - 497-504
LID - 10.1016/j.pupt.2011.07.003 [doi]
AB  - Medical imaging has been increasingly used in respiratory research due to the 
      rapid and ongoing development of medical technology. Current imaging is almost 
      entirely 3-dimensional and allows the measurement of structure and function, 
      often both simultaneously. The information complements other measurement methods 
      in airway research because imaging's greatest contributions are those of 
      topographical information, direct visualisation of functional or structural 
      change, sampling of the whole organ (in contrast to for example bronchoscopy) and 
      potential for in vivo imaging in a repeated, prospective nature. Medical imaging 
      modalities of high-resolution computed tomography (HRCT), magnetic resonance 
      imaging (MRI), ultra-high resolution imaging, positron emission computed 
      tomography (PET) and single photon emission computed tomography (SPECT) are 
      reviewed in relation to research in airways disease.
