VI  - 27
IP  - 4
DP  - 2014 Aug
TI  - Role of bronchoalveolar lavage in the diagnosis of pulmonary infiltrates in 
      immunocompromised patients.
PG  - 322-8
LID - 10.1097/QCO.0000000000000072 [doi]
AB  - PURPOSE OF REVIEW: To describe the role of bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) in the 
      evaluation of pulmonary disease in immunocompromised patients. RECENT FINDINGS: 
      Recent discoveries in this field are largely in two areas: the array of 
      diagnostic testing performed on BAL fluid and technical details that can enhance 
      the yield from this procedure. Regarding diagnostic testing, the addition of new 
      assays, including Aspergillus galactomannan antigen assay, respiratory viral 
      panels, and Pneumocystis jirovecii PCR, has improved the diagnostic yield of BAL 
      over conventional cultures and stains. To improve the diagnostic yield of the 
      procedure itself, it should be done early in the clinical course, with the BAL in 
      the anatomic area most affected, and with a preprocedural computed tomography of 
      the chest to properly plan the procedure. SUMMARY: Bronchoscopic evaluation with 
      BAL can provide important diagnostic information in immunocompromised patients 
      with pulmonary diseases and should be routinely performed when clinically 
      indicated and able to be completed safely.
