DP  - 2014 Sep
TI  - Necrotizing sarcoid granulomatosis with an uncommon manifestation: 
      clinicopathological features and review of literature.
PG  - e132-6
LID - 10.4187/respcare.02842 [doi]
AB  - We report a rare case of an incidental diagnosis of necrotizing sarcoid 
      granulomatosis (NSG) in a 60-y-old non-smoking male. The patient was admitted to 
      the hospital for sudden back pain. Chest x-ray revealed areas of parenchymal 
      consolidation and high-resolution computed tomography demonstrated a pulmonary 
      nodular pattern with no lymph node enlargement. All laboratory and pulmonary 
      function tests were normal. Bronchoscopy with bronchoalveolar lavage showed no 
      sign of infection or specific inflammation. The diagnosis of NSG was made by 
      histopathological examination of a surgical lung biopsy and by excluding other 
      causes of granulomatous disease. In paucisymptomatic/asymptomatic patients, as in 
      our case, therapy is not necessary, with a good prognosis and complete recovery. 
      NSG is a rare systemic disease similar to sarcoidosis and Wegener's 
      granulomatosis with a benign clinical course and should always be considered for 
      patients with nodular pulmonary lesions even with subclinical or uncommon 
      features.
