DP  - 2011
TI  - [Respiratory tract burn injuries].
PG  - 298-304
AB  - Respiratory tract burns are one of the most serious injuries of human organism. 
      They often accompany severe skin burns, increasing morbidity and mortality. 
      Pathologic events happening in the lungs in the course of inhalation injury 
      consist of: edema and necrosis of bronchial mucosa, increase of bronchial blood 
      flow and vascular permeability, recruitment of inflammatory mediators, and 
      obturation of bronchial tract with the casts composed of mucus, tissue debris, 
      neutrophils and fibrin. The above mentioned processes lead to progressive 
      disturbances of pulmonary gas exchange and tissue hypoxia. Introduction of 
      standardized bronchoscopic procedures resulted in the possibility of early 
      diagnosis and treatment of inhalation injuries. There are many treatment options, 
      some of them combined with early and late complications, the optimal treatment 
      protocol is still lacking. Early hyperbaric oxygen therapy is one of the most 
      promising methods of treatment leading to decrease of mortality due to inhalation 
      injury.
