TI  - Benign intrapulmonary schwannoma: aspect on F-18 fluorodeoxyglucose PET/CT.
PG  - 465-7
LID - 10.1097/RLU.0b013e31820aa268 [doi]
AB  - A 75-year-old man, with no significant symptoms, was referred after the 
      incidental finding of a left hilar pulmonary mass of 30 × 30 × 50 mm on a chest 
      CT. F-18 fluorodeoxyglucose (FDG) PET/CT demonstrated a heterogeneous, moderate 
      radiotracer uptake in the mass (SUV 3.5 g/mL). Bronchoscopy revealed a discrete 
      extrinsic compression of the superior bronchus without endobronchial lesion. 
      Endobronchial fine-needle biopsies could not deliver a final diagnosis. The 
      patient underwent upper lobectomy by thoracotomy. Histopathology revealed a 
      benign intrapulmonary schwannoma. Although rare, intermediate FDG uptake in the 
      settings of a pulmonary mass should include schwannoma in the differential 
      diagnosis.
