TI  - Solitary pulmonary nodule due to Mycobacterium kansasii.
PG  - 775-8
AB  - A 46-year-old man with no past history of underlying disease was admitted to our 
      hospital for examination of abnormal chest shadow on chest radiograph. He had no 
      respiratory symptoms on admission. Chest radiograph showed a solitary nodule (35 
      × 20 mm) in the left upper lung field. On chest CT, this nodule in the left upper 
      lobe (S(1+2)) did not demonstrate calcification, the margin was clear but 
      irregular, and there was pleural indentation. The solitary nodule was strongly 
      positive on PET/CT. Therefore, we suspected primary lung cancer. Because we could 
      not establish the diagnosis by bronchoscopic examination, video-assisted 
      thoracoscopic surgery (VATS) was performed. Histological diagnosis of the 
      solitary nodule demonstrated epitheloid granuloma with caseous necrosis. Smear 
      test of the resected tissue was positive for acid-fast bacilli and culture was 
      positive for mycobacteria, which was identified as Mycobacterium kansasii. There 
      are a few case reports of solitary nodule due to M. kansasii.
