TI  - Exhaled 8-isoprostane in sarcoidosis: relation to superoxide anion production by 
      bronchoalveolar lavage cells.
PG  - 1027-32
LID - 10.1007/s00011-010-0222-4 [doi]
AB  - OBJECTIVE: This study was designed to examine the mutual relationship between 
      8-isoprostane in exhaled breath condensate (EBC) and superoxide anion generation 
      by bronchoalveolar lavage fluid (BALF) cells in patients with sarcoidosis. 
      DESIGN: About 29 patients with sarcoidosis, 34 healthy never smokers (control 
      group for EBC) and 15 healthy never smokers (control group for BAL) were 
      examined. EBC was collected directly before bronchoscopy. 8-Isoprostane was 
      measured by ELISA, and superoxide anion by colorimetry. RESULTS: Exhaled breath 
      condensate 8-isoprostane is increased in sarcoidosis (median, 25-75 percentile): 
      2.50; 2.50-3.90 versus 6.20; 2.50-16.95 pg/ml, p ≤ 0.05). Spontaneous superoxide 
      anion release from BALF cells was significantly elevated only in patients with a 
      high percentage of lymphocytes in BALF (6.42 ± 1.24 vs. 23.52 ± 4.30 nmol/10(6) 
      cells, p ≤ 0.01). There were no correlations between 8-isoprostane and 
      spontaneous or stimulated superoxide anion release. CONCLUSIONS: We confirmed 
      higher concentrations of EBC 8-isoprostane in sarcoidosis and higher spontaneous 
      release of superoxide anion from BALF cells in patients with sarcoidosis. The 
      increase of EBC 8-isoprostane is not directly related to superoxide anion 
      released from BALF cells.
