TI  - Case report: telelaryngoscopy-guided flexible fiberoptic intubation for laryngeal 
      rhinosporidiosis.
PG  - 1066-8
LID - 10.1213/ANE.0b013e3181d2a4f2 [doi]
AB  - Rhinosporidiosis is a chronic granulomatous disease endemic in India and Sri 
      Lanka. The causative microorganism, Rhinosporidium seeberi, remains a poorly 
      understood pathogen, which has been described as an aquatic protistan parasite. 
      Rhinosporidiosis presents as multiple polypoidal lesions affecting the mucosa of 
      the nasal cavity, nasopharynx, and oropharynx. Intralaryngeal rhinosporidiosis is 
      a rare entity and poses a challenge for management of the airway. In this case 
      report, we highlight our technique in the management of the airway in a case of 
      laryngeal rhinosporidiosis using a combination of fiberoptic bronchoscope and an 
      endoscope. The technique was atraumatic and also avoided the potential for 
      autoinoculation, which is a frequent cause of recurrence of this disease.
