DP  - 2013 Winter
TI  - A survey of current practices in the diagnosis of and interventions for 
      inhalational injuries in Canadian burn centres.
PG  - 221-5
AB  - OBJECTIVE: To summarize current Canadian practice patterns in the diagnosis of 
      and interventions for inhalation injuries (INHI). METHODS: A 10-question survey 
      regarding the diagnosis of and interventions for INHI was sent to the medical 
      directors of all 16 burn centres across Canada. RESULTS: The response rate to the 
      survey was 50%. Fibreoptic bronchoscopy is required for the diagnosis of INHI in 
      only four centres (50%). The departments of intensive care, plastic surgery, 
      otolaryngology and respirology are involved in performing fibreoptic bronchoscopy 
      in 87.5%, 37.5%, 12.5% and 12.5% of Canadian burn centres, respectively. 
      Intubation for INHI is most often based on physical examination results (87.5%) 
      and clinical history (75%). The most common physical features believed to be most 
      consistent with INHI are dyspnea (87.5%) and hoarseness (87.5%). Common 
      treatments include intubation (87.5%), routine ventilatory support (87.5%) and 
      chest physiotherapy (75%). None of the centres used nebulized heparin. A total of 
      five centres (62.5%) routinely changed the fluid resuscitation protocol when INHI 
      was diagnosed. Only two centres (25%) routinely used prophylactic antibiotics for 
      INHI. CONCLUSION: Prospective, multicentre trials are needed to generate 
      evidence-based consensus in the areas of diagnosis, grading and treatment for 
      INHI in Canada.
