TI  - A case report of anesthesia for a child with Hajdu-Cheney syndrome.
PG  - 949-50
LID - 10.1007/s00540-013-1642-4 [doi]
AB  - Hajdu-Cheney syndrome is an extremely rare disorder characterized by progressive 
      skeletal acro-osteolysis, which results in extremity fractures and scoliosis 
      often requiring surgical treatment from childhood. A unique facial structure and 
      deformity of the cervical spine is associated with a difficult airway. We report 
      here a 10-year-old girl with Hajdu-Cheney syndrome who developed progressive 
      basilar impression and medullary compression for which foramen magnum 
      decompression was performed. After slow induction of anesthesia, we were able to 
      perform fiberoptic orotracheal intubation via a VBM bronchoscope airway. This 
      case report contributes to the accumulation of knowledge about anesthesia for 
      this rare syndrome.
