TI  - Difficult airway and one lung ventilation.
PG  - 477-82
AB  - The number of indications for lung collapse during different procedures is 
      rising. Difficult upper airway is more often encountered with the need for single 
      lung ventilation. In patients with difficult airway, the safest approach is by 
      placing a single-lumen endotracheal tube with the aid of a flexible fiberoptic 
      bronchoscope. Lung isolation in these patients is then achieved by means of a 
      bronchial blocker. An alternative technique is exchanging the single-lumen for a 
      double-lumen tube using an airway exchange catheter. When there is a tracheostomy 
      in place, an independent bronchial blocker is recommended.
