TI  - Multidetector computed tomography findings of central bronchopleural fistulas as 
      sequelae of tuberculosis, chemo radiation and trauma: A report of three cases.
PG  - 21-6
LID - 10.1016/j.rmcr.2013.04.001 [doi]
AB  - A bronchopleural fistula (BPF) is defined as a direct pathway between the 
      bronchial tree or lung parenchyma and the pleural space. Herein, we describe the 
      clinical findings and imaging results of BPFs in three cases. The patients' 
      medical histories revealed that the first had recurrent pulmonary tuberculosis, 
      the second had small-cell lung cancer (SCLC) and had previously undergone 
      chemoradiotherapy, and the third had trauma. Multidetector computed tomography 
      (MDCT) showed clear communication between the airways and pleural spaces which 
      was sufficient for a proper diagnosis without performing a bronchoscopy.
