DP  - 2012 Apr
TI  - Induction for classic laryngeal mask airway insertion: Does low-dose fentanyl 
      work?
PG  - 210-3
LID - 10.4103/0970-9185.94877 [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND: Laryngeal mask airway (LMA) insertion requires an optimal balance of 
      anesthesia. Propofol with different opioids is a preferred combination. Two doses 
      of fentanyl were compared for the efficacy and side effects. MATERIALS AND 
      METHODS: 96 patients were randomly distributed into F(1) (fentanyl 1 mcg/kg) and 
      F(2) (fentanyl 2 mcg/kg) groups. The conditions for LMA insertion, hemodynamic 
      profile, bronchoscopic view, and incidence of sore throat were compared. RESULT: 
      There was no statistically significant difference in any parameter in the two 
      groups except for a significant fall in systolic and mean arterial pressure in 
      F(2) group. CONCLUSION: Both doses of fentanyl (1 and 2 mcg/kg) provide 
      comparable insertion conditions for LMA. Fentanyl in the lower dose provides a 
      more stable hemodynamic profile.
