LR  - 20120227
IS  - 1526-7598 (Electronic)
IS  - 0003-2999 (Linking)
VI  - 113
IP  - 2
DP  - 2011 Aug
TI  - Airway collapse with an anterior mediastinal mass despite spontaneous ventilation 
      in an adult.
PG  - 239-42
LID - 10.1213/ANE.0b013e31821f9c95 [doi]
AB  - Patients with anterior mediastinal masses are at increased risk for perioperative 
      complications. Our case demonstrates that airway collapse and inability to 
      ventilate may occur in the asymptomatic adult despite spontaneous ventilation 
      with inhaled anesthesia and an endotracheal tube. Given the sudden and profound 
      presentation of cardiopulmonary collapse, rigid bronchoscopy should be 
      immediately available to facilitate life-saving ventilation. Though repositioning 
      the pediatric patient lateral or prone has been reported to reestablish airway 
      patency, this maneuver may be of limited benefit in the adult population because 
      of a more ossified and developed chest wall. Lastly, if a high-risk patient 
      requires a general anesthetic, strong consideration should be given to 
      preinduction placement of femoral cardiopulmonary bypass cannulae and the 
      availability to immediately initiate cardiopulmonary bypass.
FAU - Gardner, Jeffrey C
