VI  - 2013
DP  - 2013 Apr 16
TI  - Unusual case of a lung abscess.
LID - 10.1136/bcr-2012-008306 [doi]
LID - bcr2012008306
AB  - A 56-year-old Caucasian lady presented with a short history of pleuritic chest 
      pain on the background of a 2-month history of fever, chills, 10-kg weight loss 
      and cough with brown sputum after a laparoscopic cholecystectomy. She had 
      persistent eosinophilia and was diagnosed with a lung abscess as seen on chest 
      x-ray. She did not respond to standard intravenous broad spectrum antibacterial 
      medication and her chest CT scan showed a moderate pleural collection in 
      continuity with the abscess. She also underwent bronchoscopy, the microscopy of 
      the bronchial washings revealing eggs of the trematode Fasciola. The bacterial 
      and fungal cultures of the washings were sterile. She had visited Turkey in the 
      previous year but did not remember consuming any watercress or aquatic plants. 
      She was successfully treated with two doses of the antiparasitic agent 
      triclabendazole. Ectopic Fasciola can be a rare cause of a lung abscess.
