TI  - Detection of lung cancer in bronchial brushing specimens by FISH.
PG  - 679-81
LID - 10.1586/erm.12.69 [doi]
AB  - Bronchial brushing cytology specimens collected during flexible bronchoscopy are 
      an important part of the diagnostic workup of patients with indeterminate 
      pulmonary nodules. Unfortunately, false-negative diagnoses are not uncommon when 
      sampling peripheral nodules, especially small nodules <2 cm in diameter. A number 
      of studies have evaluated different FISH probe sets to increase the detection 
      rate of lung cancer on bronchial brushings and washings. Most studies have shown 
      that FISH with routine cytology increases the sensitivity of lung cancer 
      detection over routine cytology alone, while maintaining high specificity. In 
      this article, the authors review a recently published three-probe FISH assay for 
      the detection of lung cancer in bronchial brushing specimens.
