DP  - 2012 Jan-Mar
TI  - Tracheobronchial compression by right-sided aortic arch in a middle aged male.
PG  - 45-7
AB  - Exertional dyspnoea is a common symptom among middle-aged population. Diagnostic 
      evaluation of such patients is often challenging and confusing. We report a 
      patient presenting with exertional dyspnoea and an obstructive ventilatory defect 
      on spirometry that was refractory to bronchodilator therapy. Careful review of 
      the chest radiograph and spirometry pointed towards variable intra-thoracic 
      airways obstruction as a cause of dyspnoea. Contrast enhanced computed tomography 
      (CECT) of the thorax and bronchoscopy established the diagnosis of a right-sided 
      aortic arch resulting in tracheobronchial compression and tracheomalacia.
