TI  - An unusual case of foreign body aspiration mimicking cavitary tuberculosis in 
      adolescent patient: thread aspiration.
PG  - 17
AB  - Foreign body aspiration continues to be a serious problem in childhood and 
      adolescent period with significant rate of morbidity and rarely mortality. Half 
      of the foreign body aspiration cases have no history of aspiration. The main 
      foreign bodies inhaled are food fragments and different kinds of metallic 
      objects. A 12-year-old girl was referred to the pediatric pulmonology department 
      for chronic cough and hemoptysis. She had persistent infiltration and cavitary 
      lesion mimicking cavitary tuberculosis. There was no contact history with 
      tuberculosis in her family and acid resistant bacillus was not found in the 
      sputum examination. Flexible bronchoscopy was performed for persistent 
      infiltration and hemoptysis and inflamed thread was found in right lower lobe 
      bronchus. This is the first case of thread inhalation mimicking cavitary 
      tuberculosis in an adolescent patient.
