TI  - [Anesthesia for a patient with severe tracheal stenosis due to thoracic aortic 
      aneurysm].
PG  - 1195-8
AB  - Tracheobronchial compression is a well-recognized complication of thoracic aortic 
      aneurysm. We describe the anesthetic management of a patient with severe tracheal 
      stenosis due to thoracic aortic aneurysm. An 81-year-old woman was scheduled for 
      endovascular aortic stent graft placement. Computed tomographic (CT) scans showed 
      that the narrowest diameter of the trachea was 3 x 18 mm. Awake fiberoptic 
      intubation was selected for anesthesia induction, and percutaneous 
      cardiopulmonary support (PCPS) was ready to be established prior to induction of 
      anesthesia. We successfully inserted ID 6.0 mm spiral tube beyond the tracheal 
      compression using bronchoscope and induced hypotension. The operation was 
      completed successfully without any adverse events. We conclude that, in patients 
      with thoracic aortic aneurysm, careful attention should be paid not only to 
      circulation but to respiration.
