TI  - [Primary pulmonary epitheloid haemangioendothelioma].
PG  - 834-9
LID - 10.1556/OH.2011.29099 [doi]
AB  - A 49-year-old female patient was admitted in July 2009 because of cough, weight 
      loss and effort dyspnoe. Chest X-ray and CT showed multiple bilateral nodules 
      which have been identified earlier and these nodules were unchanged. However, 
      there was a new parenchymal lesion in the right upper lobe, and new right hilar 
      and mediastinal lymphadenomegaly was also found. Sample was taken by bronchoscope 
      and the pathological diagnosis was pulmonary epitheloid haemangioendothelioma. 
      This rare endothelial tumor usually affects middle-aged patients with a female 
      predominance and it presents with chest pain, effort dyspnoe, cough, sputum, or 
      it may remain asymptomatic. Multiple bilateral nodules are usually detected by 
      radiologic examination. The diagnosis of this tumor is often challenging and, 
      because of its rarity, it does not have any standard therapeutic regimen. 
      Treatment can be surgery, chemo-, radio-, hormone- or immunotherapy. In order to 
      find the most effective anticancer treatment, authors performed in vitro studies. 
      On the basis of the results, chemotherapy was initiated which resulted in a 
      partial regression.
