DP  - 2010 Jul 20
TI  - Inflammatory myofibroblastic tumor of the lung--a case report.
PG  - 55
LID - 10.1186/1749-8090-5-55 [doi]
AB  - A 45-year-old man presented with a six-month history of progressive dyspnea with 
      productive cough and wheezing. The patient was a heavy smoker and had a history 
      of tongue cancer, hypertension, and asthma. Chest X-ray and computed tomography 
      showed a mass lesion in the left hilar region and total collapse of the upper 
      left lobe of the lung. Bronchoscopy revealed a whitish solid tumor obstructing 
      the left upper lobe bronchus. Positron emission tomography showed increased 
      tracer uptake in the lesion. A thoracoscopic lobectomy of the left upper lobe of 
      the lung was performed. The final pathologic diagnosis was inflammatory 
      myofibroblastic tumor.
