TI  - Paragonimiasis in a person whose symptoms were shown 22 years after emigrating to 
      Japan from Laos.
PG  - 49-52
LID - 10.1007/s10156-009-0005-4 [doi]
AB  - We report a patient, a 52-year-old man from Laos, who had come to Japan at 30 
      years of age, but had maintained a habit of eating raw freshwater crabs. The 
      patient visited a physician for left chest pain in January 2007. Infiltration and 
      mass-like shadows were noted in the left superior and inferior lobes on chest 
      X-ray. Diagnosis could not be made by bronchial brushing, but eggs were present 
      in sputum cytology 3 days after bronchoscopy. Therefore, paragonimiasis was 
      diagnosed. The peripheral eosinophil count had increased to 2550/μl and the serum 
      IgE level was elevated, at 71000 IU/ml. Multiple-dot enzyme-linked immunosorbent 
      assay (ELISA) for specific IgG antibodies in serum was positive for Paragonimus 
      westermani and P. miyazakii. Paragonimiasis may have been caused by the style of 
      Laotian cooking without heating. Because the habit of eating raw freshwater crabs 
      is common in Laos, Laos is one of the countries where paragonimiasis is 
      prevalent. For patients from Laos with lung diseases, differentiation including 
      paragonimiasis is required.
